  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nStill quite a bit of low level moisture around this morning with\nlow stratus and fog. Eventually, heating and drier air aloft\nwill mix out the moisture and by early afternoon. However, still\nlikely to see mostly cloudy conditions this afternoon with\nwidespread cirrostratus over the area with some filtered \nsunshine. Temperatures will be seasonable in the 60s for highs.\nNortherly winds are slightly breezy between 6-15 mph through \nthe day.\n\n0Z model guidance has significantly cut back PoPs and QPF\ntonight through Tuesday. A wave of low pressure is forecast to\ntrack a bit further south and there will be a tight rainfall gradient\nbetween central PA and NE PA. Best chance for rain in our CWA\nwill be Luzerne County, but any further shift southward, as some\n0Z guidance is showing, will pull rainfall completely out of our\narea. \n\nHigh pressure to the north will be bringing in a very dry air\nmass. This high pressure system should keep the bulk of our CWA\ndry and even if rain does make it into NE PA, it will likely\nclear out by Tuesday afternoon. Temperatures will vary \nconsiderably, with cool overnight lows in the upper 30s to low 40s\nMonday night across portions Oneida County and southward into\nDelaware County, but in most locations, the lows will be in the\nmid to upper 40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure will remain over the region through the middle of\nthe week. This will bring dry conditions with cool overnight \ntemperatures into the upper 30s to low 40s. Can't completely \nrule out frost for some of the coldest spots, and this will be \nsomething to double check on as we get closer. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nForecast for the end of the week and through the weekend is\nbecoming a little more clear . A shortwave dives south out of \nQuebec into New England. At the same time a closed upper level \nlow drifts east across the Ohio Valley and off the Mid- Atlantic\ncoast. By Friday morning, phasing of these feature could occur \nand produce a strong Nor'easter off the Delmarva region. There\nstill remains some uncertainty on where this low could \nultimately develop and track, but 0Z guidance is in much better\nagreement than past model runs. For our region, at this time it\nappears there could be potential for wrap around rain to push \nback to the west into some of our eastern forecast zones Friday \ninto Saturday. Although some uncertainty remains on the overall\ndetails of the positions and track of the low, the models are \nin good agreement that a strong low pressure will be somewhere\njust off the coast. The evolution of this coastal low will \ncertainly need to be tracked closely over the coming week, as\nfurther westward shift in the track could bring more impacts to\nour region.",
